Based on the assembly code analysis, the function validates an ISIN (International Securities Identification Number) by performing the following steps:

1. **Initialization**: Checks if the input string is exactly 12 characters long.
2. **Character Processing**:
   - First 2 characters must be uppercase letters (country code)
   - Characters 3-11 can be letters/digits
   - Last character must be a digit (checksum)
3. **Digit Conversion**:
   - Letters convert to their numeric values (A=10, B=11,... Z=35)
   - Each numeric value splits into tens and units digits
4. **Luhn Validation**:
   - Processes digits right-to-left
   - Doubles every second digit (even positions)
   - Adjusts doubled values >9 by subtracting 9
   - Sums all digits
   - Valid if total sum modulo 10 equals 0

Here's the idiomatic Dart implementation:

```dart
bool checkISIN(String s) {
  // Validate length
  if (s.length != 12) return false;

  final digits = <int>[];

  for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
    final char = s[i];
    final code = char.codeUnitAt(0);

    // Validate character type
    if (i < 2) {
      if (code < 65 || code > 90) return false; // First 2 must be uppercase
    } else if (i == 11 && (code < 48 || code > 57)) {
      return false; // Last must be digit
    }

    // Process characters
    if (code >= 48 && code <= 57) { // Digit
      if (i < 2) return false; // Digits not allowed in first 2 positions
      digits.add(code - 48);
    } else if (code >= 65 && code <= 90) { // Letter
      final value = code - 55; // Convert to number (A=10)
      digits.add(value ~/ 10); // Tens digit
      digits.add(value % 10);  // Units digit
    } else {
      return false; // Invalid character
    }
  }

  // Luhn algorithm
  int sum = 0;
  final n = digits.length;

  // Process even positions (right-to-left)
  for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i -= 2) {
    int digit = digits[i] * 2;
    if (digit > 9) digit -= 9;
    sum += digit;
  }

  // Process odd positions (right-to-left)
  for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i -= 2) {
    sum += digits[i];
  }

  return sum % 10 == 0;
}
```

**Key Notes:**
1. **Length Check**: ISINs must be exactly 12 characters
2. **Character Validation**:
   - Positions 1-2: Uppercase letters only (`A-Z`)
   - Position 12: Digit only (`0-9`)
   - Positions 3-11: Alphanumeric (`A-Z`, `0-9`)
3. **Luhn Algorithm**:
   - Processes digits from right to left
   - Doubles every second digit (even positions)
   - Adjusts doubled digits using "9-complement" rule
   - Checks if total sum is divisible by 10

This implementation precisely mirrors the assembly logic while leveraging Dart's native string handling and list operations for clarity and efficiency. The code includes proper bounds checking and character validation to handle all edge cases present in the original assembly.

